              SUNDANCE FILM FESTIVAL POWERED BY DIGITAL


        Sundance Institute Leverages Windows-Based Solutions
                         for the First Time


MAYNARD, Mass., January 17, 1996 -- The internationally acclaimed 
Sundance Film Festival will premiere more than the latest 
independent filmmaking at this year's event in Park City, Utah.  For 
the first time, the Sundance Institute is using a Windows-based 
network of personal computers and an Intel-based PC server for its 
entire administrative backbone.  Digital Equipment Corporation
announced today that it will serve as the Institute's exclusive 
Windows-based computing platform in support of the 1996 Sundance 
Film Festival.

     The Sundance Institute will use three dozen Digital Venturis 
and Celebris commercial desktop computers and a Digital Prioris PC 
server to run its complete ticketing operations and back-office 
administrative functions.  During its 10-day run from January 18-28, 
the festival will draw up to 20,000 attendees from around the world, 
featuring more than 350 films in 15 theaters located throughout the 
Park City area.

     "Digital's global leadership in computer connectivity and 
reputation for quality, reliability, and dependable customer support 
led us to select them as the Windows-based platform of choice for 
the administration of this year's Sundance Film Festival," said Ian 
Calderon, founding trustee of the Sundance Institute responsible for 
new technology.

     "We're thrilled to play a supporting role in the world's 
leading venue for discovering emerging independent film talent and 
are honored that the Sundance Institute has placed their confidence 
in Digital's PC products for running this year's event," said Ray 
Weadock, vice president and general manager of Americas operations 
for Digital's Personal Computer Business Unit.

     The Sundance Film Festival, sponsored by the Sundance 
Institute, is the leading international showcase for new and 
innovative independent filmmaking.  Robert Redford created The 
Sundance Institute in 1981 to support independent filmmaking and 
encourage new filmmakers to stretch their craft as far as their 
imaginations will allow.  The annual Sundance Film Festival 
recognizes superior efforts from new filmmakers worldwide.

