                            BACKGROUND FOR EDITORS

                                 January 1996


          DIGITAL'S ALPHA SYSTEMS FOR HIGH-PERFORMANCE 3-D ANIMATION
        

Introduction

Softimage is a provider of design software for the creation of 
animations, and promotional stills, in the Entertainment, 
Advertising, Computer Gaming and Design industries.

Until two years ago it was an independent companies started in 
Montreal by Danielle Langois and Claude Cajolet. It was acquired by 
Microsoft along with Rendermorphics to position Microsoft as the 
industry leader for 3D graphics software.

Softimage's principal competition comes from Alias and Wavefront 
which produce similar and competing products. The principal hardware 
platform of choice was SGI, however when Softimage was purchased the 
strategic direction become Windows NT.

For the initial launch of the the Softimage 3D product they have 
qualified three Windows NT platforms. These are Intergraph, 
NetPower, and Digital.


Digital's Hardware Platforms

Digital brings the unsurpassed power of Alpha technology to the 
Softimage marketplace. By utilizing the joint engineering and 
marketing of the Microsoft/Digital Alliance Digital is able to offer 
the best solutions at superior price/performance.

Introduced in July, 1995, the AlphaStation 600 5/266 is Digital's 
highest performing workstation.  The AlphaStation 600 5/266 is 
powered by the Alpha 21164 RISC microprocessor, the industry's first 
to process more than one billion instructions per second.  With up 
to 1-Gbyte of ECC memory and a 64-bit PCI I/O bus at 
264-Mbytes/second, the AlphaStation 600 5/266 delivers the best in 
rendering performance. 

The AlphaStation 600 5/266 workstation is one member of Digital's 
fill line of powerful 64-bit AlphaStation workstations which range 
in price from $5,000 to $50,000. The family includes the entry-level 
AlphaStation 200 systems; the mid-range AlphaStation 250 
workstation; and the AlphaStation 600 system, the world's fastest 
workstation. 

AlphaStation workstations are ideally suited for applications in 
mechanical computer-aided design, electrical computer-aided design, 
geographic information systems, software development, and science 
and research. The combination of raw processing power and the wide 
range of graphics options that are available for AlphaStation 
workstations represent a new superpower workstation computing.

The Alpha XL 266 is a member of Digital's Personal Workstation 
family, which offers customers an enormous range of 
price/performance options.  The family features superb expandability 
with the high-performance PCI bus running at 132 megabytes per 
second for fast graphics, SCSI, and networking options.  For 
graphics, standard configurations feature the Matrox Millennium 
Graphics Adapter, and options include the S3 Diamond Stealth and 
AccelGraphics 3D graphics accelerators.  Digital also offers its own 
3D graphics adapter - the ZLXp-L1 card - for high performance 3D 
graphics.

The Digital Personal Workstations are sold exclusively through 
indirect channels, most specifically through Value Added Resellers 
(VARs).  Digital builds the base platform, installs the Windows NT 
operating System, and ships the product to distributors such as 
Hallmark, Gates/Arrow, Pioneer, and Wyle.  These partners resell to 
various VARs who, in turn, add their software and service components 
and deliver the solution to the end user.

Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
