                DIGITAL POWERS PAST THE COMPETITION;
                SHIPS 1000th AlphaServer 8000 SYSTEM


    ... Japanese bank bets its data warehouse future on Alpha ...


       ... Market demand validates the power and affordability
                       of 64-bit computing ...


MAYNARD, Mass., January 24, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced that in just seven months it has shipped 1,000 
high-end AlphaServer 8000 series enterprise systems, the industry's 
most powerful servers. 

     The 1000th AlphaServer 8000 customer is Mitsui Trust and 
Banking Co., Ltd., one of Japan's leading financial houses and a 
member of the Mitsui Group. Mitsui Trust has installed a Digital 
UNIX AlphaServer 8400 symmetric multiprocessing (SMP) system for a 
data warehousing application using the Oracle7 database and SAS 
Institute software.

     According to Pauline Nist, Digital vice president of the 
AlphaServer Business Segment at Digital, "Since the first 
AlphaServer 8000 system went out the door last June, we have already 
shipped more systems than the IBM RS/6000 Scalable POWERparallel 
System, which has been shipping for nearly three years. This 
milestone is one more indication of the explosive market enthusiasm 
for our 64-bit Alpha systems."  

AlphaServer Systems -- The World's Most Powerful Servers

     The AlphaServer 8400 delivers the power, throughput, and 
balanced processor and I/O performance to handle the most demanding 
large commercial, technical, and multi-user applications. 

     AlphaServer systems hold world database transaction records for 
a single system solution and single-CPU solution, and for leading 
databases, including Oracle7, Sybase System 11, and Microsoft SQL 
Server, as well as for the SAP R/3 application suite and a number of 
Internet and other applications. 

     Today's power-hungry applications, such as data warehousing, 
OLTP, simulation, and modeling, as well as Internet and general 
business applications, are driving demand for the performance and 
fully-available 64-bit very large memory (VLM64) computing 
environment offered only by Alpha. 

     Only Digital is shipping the high-performance 64-bit servers, 
AlphaServer systems, and a 64-bit operating system, Digital UNIX, 
which together make VLM64 possible.  Digital's VLM64 technology 
dramatically improves database and application performance by 
raising the amount of information that can be stored in memory. All 
32-bit systems are limited to 2GB of memory. Today AlphaServer 8400 
systems can store up to 14GB of data in memory, with more in the 
future.

Demonstrating the Dramatic Growth of AlphaServer Systems

     In announcing the AlphaServer 8000 milestone, Nist outlined a 
host of other recent developments in the AlphaServer line-up that 
offer compelling evidence of the power, scalability, and 
affordability of Digital's premier computing solutions: 

     o	Major customers, such as American Credit Indemnity, Baxter 
        Healthcare, Best Western Hotels, Genentech, Milwaukee 
        Sentinel-Journal, Mitel, Polish National Railway, Reuben H. 
        Donnelley, Royal Hong Kong Jockey Club, the State University 
        of New York, and Wells Fargo Bank are moving to Alpha for 
        their line-of-business applications. The list includes 
        high-profile companies and small start-ups in virtually 
        every market, such as telecommunications, finance, science, 
        healthcare, retailing, and government. 

     o	All the major database vendors, including Informix, Oracle, 
        Software AG, and Sybase, now support Digital's VLM64 
        capabilities to offer their customers breakthrough database 
        performance.  

     o	Sixty percent of AlphaServer 8000 systems are shipping with 
        the Digital UNIX operating system; the balance are shipping 
        with the OpenVMS OS. Both feature full 64-bit capability. 
        While other major vendors are struggling to introduce one 
        64-bit OS, Digital is already supplying two.

     o	A significant number of the AlphaServer 8000 shipments are 
        in the fast-growing data warehousing market. The AlphaServer 
        was recently certified as one of the industry's first 
        platforms to earn the Oracle Warehouse Test-to-Scale 
        certification. 

     o	A recent reader satisfaction survey by Computerworld reveals 
        that in all five categories -- batch performance, on-line 
        transaction performance, reliability, scalability, and 
        service and support -- more readers give their Digital 
        high-end AlphaServer systems "very good" marks than those 
        with competitive systems from HP, IBM, and Sun. 

     o	Digital recently introduced the first wave of products and 
        services that facilitate the integration of OpenVMS and 
        Windows NT.  These capabilities make it increasingly easy to 
        develop, deploy, integrate and manage applications across 
        both platforms in a three-tier client/server environment.

     o	In December Digital announced new technology -- a PCI to 
        Memory Channel interconnect -- that enables its highly 
        scalable AlphaServer systems to deliver new levels of 
        affordability and price/performance for the technical 
        computing market. 

     o	In December the company opened the doors to its new Database 
        Technology Center (DBTC), located in Palo Alto, Calif. The 
        DBTC offers world-class database technology expertise 
        designed to help customers capitalize on the performance and 
        productivity advantages afforded by Digital's VLM64 
        technology and that of its database software partners. 

     Digital Equipment Corporation is the world's leader in open 
client/server solutions from personal computing to integrated 
worldwide information systems.  Digital's scalable Alpha platforms, 
storage, networking, software and services, together with industry-
focused solutions from business partners, help organizations compete 
and win in today's global marketplace. 
