           DIGITAL'S NEW HIGH-END AlphaStation RAISES ANTE
                  IN ENGINEERING WORKSTATION MARKET


       ...New workstation outperforms offerings from Sun, HP,
                   and SGI by up to 200 percent...


     ...Fastest mid-range graphics in the industry now available
                 for Alpha-based NT workstations...


MAYNARD, Mass., January 29, 1996, -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today introduced its latest AlphaStation workstation that once again 
dwarfs the performance capabilities of its competitors by as much as 
200 percent in key technical benchmarks.  The workstation industry's 
new performance and price/performance leader, the AlphaStation 600 
5/333 delivers unprecedented performance for thousands of the most 
demanding technical applications.

     Incorporating the newest 64-bit Alpha microprocessor -- the 
powerful 333 MHz 21164 Alpha chip -- the AlphaStation 600 5/333 runs 
the most popular technical software applications, including 
offerings from Parametric Technology, EDS/Unigraphics, Mentor 
Graphics, Synopsys, Softimage, ESRI, and many others. And because 
AlphaStations are chip and board upgradeable, technical end users 
requiring the highest performance levels can easily upgrade to the 
AlphaStation 600 5/333.

     The AlphaStation 600 5/333 is the fastest workstation on the 
market with record-breaking SPECint95 performance of 9.19 and 
SPECfp95 performance of 13.2. It is also is the world's first 
workstation to break the 400 SPECint92 barrier with 412.4 SPECint92 
and to offer a leading price/performance ratio of $84.9/SPECint92. 
For floating point, it delivers the outstanding performance of 545.2 
SPECfp92. The superior integer and floating point capabilities make 
the new workstation an optimal platform for complex compute- and 
graphics-intensive design applications. 

     "The AlphaStation 600 5/333 workstation rewrites the playbook 
for high-performance workstations," said William Glazier, 
workstation marketing director. "For engineering organizations, 
where time-to-market separates the winners from the losers, this 
workstation is the answer. The AlphaStation 600 5/333 is another big 
step forward in Digital's objective to capture market share in the 
technical workstation market." 

The AlphaStation 600 5/333 workstation provides:

     o 	63 percent more performance for 31 percent better 
        price/performance than Sun Microsystems' Ultra 1 170E;

     o 	195 percent more performance and 128 percent better 
        price/performance than Hewlett-Packard's J200;

     o 	and 134 percent more performance and 109 percent better 
        price/performance than Silicon Graphics, Inc.'s (SGI) Indigo 
        2 250, the currently shipping high-end workstation from SGI. 

     All comparisons are based on SPECint92 and $/SPECint92, 
industry standard performance metrics for graphics workstations. 

Lightning Fast Mid-range Graphics for Windows NT 

     For customers who want to run powerful graphics applications
under the Windows NT operating system, Digital announced the 
industry's best midrange 3D graphics accelerators -- the ZLXp-L1 and 
ZLXp-L2 boards.  With the same outstanding graphics performance 
found in UNIX environments, the L1 and L2 graphics adapters are 
ideal for mechanical computer-aided design (MCAD) applications such 
as Parametric Technology's PRO/Engineer; 3D animation software such 
as Microsoft's Softimage; and many others.

     "Digital announced the ZLXp-L1 and ZLXp-L2 3D graphics boards 
for the UNIX environment seven months ago, and they're still 
industry leaders in the mid-range," Glazier continued. "Now 
customers can get the same outstanding level of graphics performance 
on sophisticated applications for Windows NT."

     The ZLXp-L1 graphics adapter beats competitors on key 
applications benchmarks. An AlphaStation 600 5/333 workstation with 
L1 graphics provides 100 percent better performance and 40 percent 
better price/performance than SGI's Indigo II 250 workstation with 
an Extreme graphics adapter running rendering tests on Microsoft's 
Softimage.

Pricing and Availability

     Available immediately, the AlphaStation 600 5/333 workstation 
is $28,885 (U.S. list) for a Windows NT-based version that includes 
a 17-inch color monitor, a 1 Gbyte disk, 4 MBytes fast cache, 32 
MBytes of memory, a ZLXp-E1 graphics adapter, floppy, CD-ROM, audio, 
mouse, keyboard, and a an industry leading three-year warranty. 
AlphaStation workstations also run the Digital UNIX and OpenVMS 
operating systems. 

     In addition, Digital has reduced the price of the AlphaStation 
600 5/266 workstation. A Windows NT-based version is $23,885 for a 
package that includes a 17-inch color monitor, a 1 Gbyte disk, 2 
MBytes fast cache, 32 MBytes of memory, a ZLXp-E1 graphics adapter, 
floppy, CD-ROM, audio, mouse, keyboard, and a three-year warranty.  

Burgeoning Applications Portfolio

     AlphaStation workstations are especially well suited for use in 
mechanical computer-aided design, electrical computer-aided design, 
geographic information systems, software development, and science 
and research. Digital's portfolio of applications now includes 
