             DIGITAL ANNOUNCES INDUSTRY'S FIRST CARDBUS-
              COMPLIANT 10/100Mbps ETHERNET CONTROLLER


 ...21142 Chip Also Targets Motherboards With Upgradable Solution...


MAYNARD, Mass., January 29, 1996 -- Digital Equipment Corporation 
today announced the industry's first Cardbus-compliant 10/100 
megabit-per-second (Mbps) Ethernet controller.  The 21142 chip, the 
latest addition to Digital's family of PCI Ethernet/Fast Ethernet 
controllers, is also the industry's first 10/100Mbps device 
optimized for motherboard applications.

     The 21142 Fast Ethernet controller offers users a low-cost, 
easily upgradable network solution.  It contains a complete, 
single-chip 10Mbps implementation and the provision to add 
10/100Mbps capability through a media-independent interface (MII).  
A thin quad flat pack (TQFP) option and 32-bit interface provides 
Cardbus compliance for mobile and portable PCMCIA applications.  A 
plastic quad flat pack (PQFP) option is also available for 
motherboard applications.

     With device drivers to support all major PC operating systems, 
the 21142 chip offers a flexible network solution for both portable 
and PCI-based PCs, as well as servers, communication routers, and 
switching products.

Answers Network Needs

     "Networking has become very important in mobile computing," 
said Deborah Vogt, marketing manager for Digital Semiconductor's 
Network Products Group.  "The 32-bit Cardbus is bringing high-
performance networking to portable and mobile systems.  The 21142 
controller's 32-bit interface accommodates both Cardbus and PCI, 
enabling developers to offer a 10Mbps Ethernet solution for 
base-level mobile and desktop systems, with an upgrade path to 
100Mbps Fast Ethernet capability when the user is ready.

     "Analysts predict that the demand for network-ready PCs will 
make Ethernet a standard feature on motherboards this year," Vogt 
added.  "As the first 10/100Mbps Ethernet controller optimized for 
the motherboard, the 21142 chip offers the market a 100Mbps-ready 
solution that can be easily configured to meet customers' needs."

Integral Transceiver, AUI Port

     The 21142 chip's full 10Mbps implementation includes an 
integrated 10BaseT transceiver and attachment unit interface (AUI) 
port.  The MII, for 10/100BaseT operation, supports all media types.  
Like the 21140A PCI Fast Ethernet controller, the 3.3V 21142 chip 
complies with the PCI V2.1 specification to allow subvendor ID, 
accommodates both 5.0V and 3.3V signaling, features power-saving 
snooze and sleep modes, and provides support for network speed 
autodetection, as well as boot ROM and JTAG boundary-scan test 
support.

     The 21142 chip functions as a PCI bus master and supports 
direct memory access (DMA) data transfers at burst rates up to 
200Mbps.  DMA capability eliminates transfer bottlenecks and frees 
the CPU from involvement in data transfers. Programmable burst size 
permits efficient use of the PCI bus with minimal transaction overhead.  
Large, on-chip, receive and transmit FIFO (First In, First Out) 
buffers support full duplex operation and avoid the need for off-chip 
memory.

Pricing, Availability

     The 21142 PCI Fast Ethernet Controller chip is priced at $22.70 
in the TQFP package, and at $19.10 in the PQFP package.  Both prices 
are for quantities of 5,000-units.  Samples are available now; volume 
shipments will begin in March.  A 21142 Evaluation Kit containing a 
10/100Mbps PCI Ethernet evaluation board, PC operating system drivers 
and comprehensive documentation, will be available for $495.

     Digital Semiconductor, a Digital Equipment Corporation business 
headquartered in Hudson, Massachusetts, designs, manufactures and 
markets industry-leading semiconductor products including Alpha 
microprocessors and PCI chips for networking, bridging, and 
graphics/multimedia, as well as low-power StrongARM microprocessors 
under license from Advanced RISC Machines Ltd.  Mitsubishi Electric 
Company is a second source for Alpha microprocessors.  World Wide Web 
site:  http://www.digital.com/info/semiconductor

